  "narrative": "In the 2020s, Black and African women's hairstyles and hair culture are a vibrant tapestry of self-expression and identity. The natural hair movement continues to thrive, with women embracing their textured locks in all its forms - waves, curls, kinks, and more. Hairstyles like the afro, cornrows, and box braids have become iconic symbols of African diasporic culture, while locs (dreadlocks) are a testament to the versatility of natural hair. Protective hairstyles like Bantu knots and box braids are also gaining popularity, as women seek to minimize manipulation and exposure to environmental stressors that can damage their hair. These hairstyles not only enhance beauty but also serve as a means of resistance against centuries of Eurocentric standards of beauty. While documentation from this era is limited, it's clear that Black and African women's hair culture remains a powerful force in shaping identity, community, and self-love.",
